course: adult-life
lesson: 32
title: Bad news, protests and helping others
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The news shows the most urgent and often the worst things happening anywhere in the world, so it can feel as if everything is going wrong. Grown-ups worry about it too, and sometimes they switch it off to protect you and themselves.
explain: Not everything you see is true: some stories and videos are made up or edited on purpose to fool people. Good questions are: who made it, where else is it reported, and is it trying to make me angry?
explain: When people want something changed, they can protest peacefully, sign a petition, or stop work together in a strike. When they want to help, they give money to charities, get sponsored for a challenge or volunteer their time.
explain: Scene: on a Saturday in Toronto, Gran heads off to her shift at the charity shop, your cousin texts asking you to sponsor her 5k run for the hospice, and outside the station a man in a sleeping bag asks for spare change. Mum gives him a smile and buys him a coffee. Families help in different ways, and none of them is wrong.
fact: The news mostly reports the worst and most urgent events, because that is what feels important to report.
fact: Parents may switch off the news to protect children from scary pictures, and they worry about it themselves.
fact: Fake news means made-up stories or edited pictures spread on purpose to fool people; some people also use the phrase for news they simply dislike.
fact: A war is when countries or groups fight with weapons, and ordinary people get hurt and lose their homes; many people help by giving to charities.
fact: A protest is people gathering to show they strongly agree or disagree with something; in free countries peaceful protest is a right.
fact: A petition is a list of names of people asking for a change; the more people sign it, the harder it is for those in charge to ignore.
fact: A strike is when workers stop working together to push for better pay or conditions, and it is legal if they follow the rules.
fact: A charity is an organisation that helps people, animals or the environment, paid for by money the public gives.
fact: Sponsorship means people promise to give money to a charity if someone completes a challenge.
fact: Volunteering means working without pay to help a good cause, like Gran's shift at the charity shop.
fact: A homeless person has nowhere safe to live, often because of losing a job, a family breaking up or illness.
fact: Some grown-ups give money to homeless people, some buy food, and some give to homeless charities; none of these is wrong.
exercise: Dad switches off the news: «Not now, it's all bad news.» Why? | He wants to protect you from scary pictures | The news is only for grown-ups by law | He is cross with you for coming in without knocking first | The TV is about to break down
exercise: Big brother says a video is «fake news». What does that mean? | It is made up or edited to fool people | It is real news from a famous channel | It is an old video from last year | It is a funny cartoon for children
exercise: Mum goes quiet at pictures of bombed buildings. What is happening in the world? | There is a war, and ordinary people are hurt | A film is being made about old buildings in a city | Builders are knocking down empty houses | There has been a big storm at the seaside
exercise: Crowds on TV with banners, chanting in the street. What is it? | A protest about something they care about | A football match that ended badly | A queue for a new shop opening | A school trip that got lost in the middle of town
exercise: Mum signs something on her phone: «If it gets 100,000 signatures, they have to debate it.» What is it? | A petition asking for a change | A birthday card for her best friend | A form to buy a new mobile phone | A bill she has to pay this week
exercise: «Train drivers are on strike again tomorrow.» What does that mean? | They stop work together for better pay or conditions | They are all going on a long holiday together tomorrow | They have been sacked from their jobs | They are driving the trains extra fast
exercise: A charity tin rattles outside the supermarket. What is the money for? | A charity's work helping people, animals or nature | The supermarket's new self-service tills by the door | The tin holder's own shopping | The council's parking meters
exercise: Your cousin asks: «Sponsor me, I'm running 5k for the hospice.» What happens if you sponsor her? | You give money to the hospice when she finishes | You run the 5k instead of her | She pays you a pound for every kilometre she manages to run | You get a medal from the hospice
exercise: Gran: «I'm off to do my shift at the charity shop.» Is she paid? | No, she volunteers to help a good cause | Yes, she gets paid by the hour like any shop worker | Yes, she keeps the money she takes | No, because she is being punished
exercise: A man in a sleeping bag outside the station asks for change. What is probably true? | He is homeless and has nowhere safe to live | He is camping there for fun | He is waiting for a late train | He is collecting money for his own charity in the doorway
exercise: Your family gives to a homeless charity instead of giving coins. Is that wrong? | No, families help in different ways | Yes, you must always give coins | Yes, charities never help anyone | No, but only if you give every day
exercise: You heard something scary on the news. What is best? | Ask a grown-up about it | Keep it to yourself and worry | Watch more news alone to find out | Tell your little brother it's coming
exercise: How should you protest if you care about a cause? | Peacefully, without hurting anyone | By breaking windows so they have to listen | By shouting at strangers on the buses | You can't, all protests are banned here
exercise: Do you have to give money every time you see a charity tin? | No, you can choose causes you care about | Yes, it is the law to give something every single time | Yes, or the charity shop closes | No, children are never allowed to give
exercise: Can children volunteer too? | Yes, for example litter picking or helping at a fair | No, only retired people are allowed to volunteer anywhere | Yes, but only if they are paid | No, it is against the law under 18
exercise: Why do strikes cause problems for the public? | Because stopping work is what gives workers bargaining power | Because the workers secretly want to annoy their own families | Because the government orders them to stop | Because trains are too old to run
test: Your sister says «Fake news means any news Dad doesn't like.» Put her right. | It means made-up stories spread on purpose, though some use it that way | Yes, that is exactly and only what it means, so you can ignore it | It means news that is more than a week old | It means news read out by a robot voice
test: A video says «Schools closing forever from Monday!!» but no news site reports it. What should you think? | It's probably fake, so check before believing it | It must be true because it's a video and videos cannot lie | It's true, but only for your school | Share it fast so everyone knows
test: Overheard: «Bloody trains, strike again. Mind you, I'd strike too on that pay.» What does he think? | Annoyed by the strike but understands why | Happy the trains are not running | Wants to become a train driver | Thinks strikes are against the law
test: Kid on the bus: «Protests are always riots, innit.» Is he right? | No, most protests are calm and peaceful | Yes, every protest turns into a riot | Yes, protests are always illegal | No, protests are only on TV shows
test: Mum after the news: «Those poor families, I've sent twenty quid to the Red Cross.» Why did she do it? | To help bring food and medicine to people in a war | To pay for her own train ticket home | To buy a raffle ticket for herself to win the big hamper | To pay a fine she had forgotten
test: Your cousin did a sponsored silence for an hour and raised £40. Where does the money go? | To the charity she chose | Into her own pocket money | To the school's head teacher | To the people who sponsored her
test: Brother: «Homeless people are just lazy, don't look at him.» What's the fair answer? | People become homeless for many reasons, and he deserves respect | He's right, all homeless people are lazy and could get a job tomorrow | Homeless people choose it as a holiday | Looking at him will make him angry
test: Dad turns the radio off: «Enough doom for one day.» What is he doing? | Taking a break from heavy news | Trying to save the radio's batteries | Getting ready to go to bed at noon | Telling you the radio is broken
test: Mum's petition to save the town library gets thousands of signatures. What does a big petition do? | It shows those in charge how many people want the change | It turns into a new law straight away | It is thrown away unread, because nobody has time to read so many names | It makes the Prime Minister resign
test: Gran says «I don't get a penny, love, but I get a cuppa and good company.» What does she do? | Volunteers in the charity shop | Works there for a big wage | Owns the charity shop | Sells her own clothes for cash
test: Little brother is scared after seeing a war on the news. What can you tell him? | It's far away, and grown-ups are working to help | It's coming to our street tomorrow, so we must pack our bags | Wars only happen in films | Don't tell Mum, she'll get cross
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a strike? | A collective cessation of work in pursuit of improved pay or conditions | A punishment imposed upon workers by the government for poor performance | A holiday granted to employees by their employer | An unlawful act in every circumstance whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a petition. | To demonstrate the number of persons requesting a particular change | To impose a new law without any further deliberation | To collect funds on behalf of a registered charity | To record the votes cast at a general election
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning volunteering. | The performance of work without remuneration in aid of a good cause | The performance of work in return for a substantial salary | The obligatory service imposed upon retired persons by law | The sale of one's personal possessions for private profit
exam: It is given that a widely circulated video is reported by no reputable source. Determine the most prudent course of action. | Verify its origin before accepting it as true | Distribute it forthwith to all of one's acquaintances | Accept it as accurate on account of its popularity | Report it forthwith to the police as a criminal offence
